     yyppllddaapp..ccoonnff — LDAP YP map daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ypldap(8) daemon provides YP maps using LDAP as a backend.

     The yyppllddaapp..ccoonnff config file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for ypldap(8).

     _D_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_i_e_s
           LDAP Directory specific parameters.

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, ddoommaaiinn).  Within unquoted arguments, the string $_n_a_m_e
     is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           fixed_gecos="Pulled from LDAP"

           fixed attribute gecos $fixed_gecos

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Global settings concern the main behaviour of the daemon.

     ddoommaaiinn _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the name of the NIS domain ypldap(8) will provide.
     iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Specify the interval in seconds at which the whole directory will
             be pulled from LDAP.
     pprroovviiddee mmaapp _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify a map that should be provided by ypldap(8) The currently
             implemented maps are: passwd.byname, passwd.byuid, group.byname,
             group.bygid.
     ccaaffiillee _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Load CA certificates from the specified file to validate the
             server certificate.  If not specified, CA certificates will be
             loaded from _/_e_t_c_/_s_s_l_/_c_e_r_t_._p_e_m.
     bbiinndd _m_o_d_e
             Specify how the domain is made available for binding.  Valid op‐
             tions are:

             ppoorrttmmaapp  Register with portmap(8) and allow ypbind(8) discovery.
                      This is the default mode.

             llooccaall    Create a YP binding file in _/_v_a_r_/_y_p_/_b_i_n_d_i_n_g to enable YP
                      support in the passwd(5) and group(5) databases.  In
                      this mode it is not necessary to run portmap(8), and
                      ypbind(8) must not be running.  YP services are only
                      available to the host running ypldap(8).

   DDiirreeccttoorriieess
     Directories are used to describe the LDAP schema and help ypldap(8) con‐
     vert LDAP entries to passwd(5), master.passwd(5), and group(5) lines.
     Each directory section consists of a declaration of the directory server
     name and a set of directives describing how entries from the directory
     are used to construct YP map entries.

     ddiirreeccttoorryy _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e [ppoorrtt _p_o_r_t] [tls] {...}
             Defines a directory by hostname and optionally port number.  If
             the _t_l_s argument is not specified, no transport-level security
             will be used.  Valid options are:

             ttllss     Use STARTTLS to negotiate TLS, by default on port 389.

             llddaappss   Connect with TLS enabled, by default on port 636.

     Valid directives for directories are:

     aattttrriibbuuttee _n_a_m_e mmaappss ttoo _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Map the passwd(5), master.passwd(5), or group(5) attribute to the
             LDAP attribute name supplied.

     bbaasseeddnn _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the supplied search base as starting point for the directory
             search.

     cceerrttffiillee _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the specified client certificate when connecting to the di‐
             rectory.  The file must contain a PEM encoded certificate.

     ggrroouuppddnn _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the supplied search base as starting point for the directory
             search for groups.  If not supplied, the basedn value will be
             used.

     bbiinnddccrreedd _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the supplied credentials for simple authentication against
             the directory.

     bbiinnddddnn _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the supplied Distinguished Name to bind to the directory.

     bbiinnddeexxtt [_s_t_r_i_n_g]
             Bind to the directory using SASL EXTERNAL, optionally using a
             supplied identity string.  When using a TLS client certificate,
             this allows the client to bind as the subject of the certificate.
             If an identity string is supplied, usually in the form of a dis‐
             tinguished name prefixed with "dn:", the directory will only al‐
             low the bind to succeed if it matches the subject of the certifi‐
             cate.

     ffiixxeedd aattttrriibbuuttee _a_t_t_r_i_b_u_t_e _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Do not retrieve the specified attribute from LDAP but instead set
             it unconditionally to the supplied value for every entry.

     ggrroouupp ffiilltteerr _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the supplied LDAP filter to retrieve group entries.

     kkeeyyffiillee _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the specified private key when connecting to the directory.
             The file must contain a PEM encoded key.

     lliisstt _n_a_m_e mmaappss ttoo _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Map the passwd(5), master.passwd(5), or group(5) attribute to the
             LDAP attribute name supplied.  A list creates a comma separated
             list of all the LDAP attributes found.

             Valid attributes are:

                   nnaammee
                   ppaasssswwdd
                   uuiidd
                   ggiidd
                   ggeeccooss
                   hhoommee
                   sshheellll
                   cchhaannggee
                   eexxppiirree
                   ccllaassss
                   ggrroouuppnnaammee
                   ggrroouuppppaasssswwdd
                   ggrroouuppggiidd
                   ggrroouuppmmeemmbbeerrss

     ppaasssswwdd ffiilltteerr _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Use the supplied LDAP filter to retrieve password entries.

